The class below is a Durable Object. It runs one instance per room name, and each instance processes its requests one at a time, so writes to a room are serialized.

```typescript
import { DurableObject } from "cloudflare:workers";

export interface Env {
  CHAT_ROOM: DurableObjectNamespace<ChatRoom>;
}

const HOUR_MS = 60 * 60 * 1000;
const RETENTION_MS = 30 * 24 * HOUR_MS;

export interface ChatMessage {
  id: number;
  author: string;
  body: string;
  sent_at: number;
}

export class ChatRoom extends DurableObject<Env> {
  constructor(ctx: DurableObjectState, env: Env) {
    super(ctx, env);
    ctx.blockConcurrencyWhile(async () => {
      this.ctx.storage.sql.exec(`
        CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS messages (
          id      INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT,
          author  TEXT    NOT NULL,
          body    TEXT    NOT NULL,
          sent_at INTEGER NOT NULL
        );
        CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS messages_sent_at ON messages (sent_at);
      `);
      if ((await this.ctx.storage.getAlarm()) === null) {
        await this.ctx.storage.setAlarm(Date.now() + HOUR_MS);
      }
    });
  }

  async postMessage(author: string, body: string): Promise<ChatMessage> {
    return this.ctx.storage.sql
      .exec<ChatMessage>(
        "INSERT INTO messages (author, body, sent_at) VALUES (?, ?, ?) RETURNING *",
        author,
        body,
        Date.now(),
      )
      .one();
  }

  async getMessages(limit = 50, beforeId?: number): Promise<ChatMessage[]> {
    const rows = beforeId === undefined
      ? this.ctx.storage.sql.exec<ChatMessage>(
          "SELECT * FROM messages ORDER BY id DESC LIMIT ?", limit)
      : this.ctx.storage.sql.exec<ChatMessage>(
          "SELECT * FROM messages WHERE id < ? ORDER BY id DESC LIMIT ?", beforeId, limit);
    return rows.toArray().reverse();
  }

  async alarm(): Promise<void> {
    this.ctx.storage.sql.exec(
      "DELETE FROM messages WHERE sent_at < ?",
      Date.now() - RETENTION_MS,
    );
    await this.ctx.storage.setAlarm(Date.now() + HOUR_MS);
  }
}
```

To call it from your Worker, use `env.CHAT_ROOM.getByName(roomId).postMessage(author, body)`.

Add this to your Wrangler config:

```jsonc
"durable_objects": { "bindings": [{ "name": "CHAT_ROOM", "class_name": "ChatRoom" }] },
"migrations": [{ "tag": "v1", "new_sqlite_classes": ["ChatRoom"] }]
```

You didn't say what the hourly wake-up should do, so `alarm()` deletes messages older than 30 days and then sets the next alarm. Put your own work there instead. The first alarm is set the first time a room is used. After that, each alarm sets the next one.

I read the durable-objects skill and wrote the `ChatRoom` class from it. I didn't add the class to any file in the repo or call any Cloudflare tools, because there was nothing to deploy.
